<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eliza Lucas Pinckney</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1722">1722</a>–<a href="/years/1793">1793</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eliza Lucas Pinckney was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> planter who, while managing her father's plantations as a young woman, developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> as a commercial crop in the 1740s. Indigo, grown by enslaved workers, became one of the colony's chief exports and greatly enriched the Lowcountry planter elite. Her sons Charles Cotesworth Pinckney and Thomas Pinckney became leading figures of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Elizabeth Lucas was born on 28 December 1722 on Antigua, in the British Leeward Islands, and grew up on one of her family's three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> plantations there. She was the eldest child of Lieutenant Colonel George Lucas of the British Army. Like her brothers she was sent to school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where she studied French and music and said that botany was her favourite subject. In 1738 her father moved the family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>, where he had inherited three plantations, and they settled at Wappoo, on a tidal creek about 17 miles by land from Charleston.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1739 Colonel Lucas returned to Antigua, where he became lieutenant governor, and at 16 Eliza took charge of Wappoo and its 20 enslaved workers while supervising the overseers of the other two plantations. Her father sent her seeds to try, and after experimenting with ginger, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, alfalfa and hemp she turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> from 1739. After three years of failed attempts she showed that it could be grown and processed in the colony, succeeding best with an indigo maker of African descent whom her father hired from the French West Indies.</p><p>She used her 1744 crop to make seed and shared it with other planters. Exports of the dye rose from 5,000 pounds in 1745–46 to 130,000 pounds by 1748. On 25 May 1744 she married the widower Charles Pinckney, a lawyer and politician who had been Speaker of the Commons House of Assembly, having refused two suitors her father proposed. The family lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> for five years from 1753; soon after their return in 1758 Charles died of malaria, and she managed both families' plantations as a widow for more than 30 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She died of cancer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in 1793, where she had gone for treatment, and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> was a pallbearer at her funeral in St. Peter's Church. She had copied her letters into a letter-book from her arrival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>, most of it covering 1739 to 1762; handed down from mother to daughter, it was given to the South Carolina Historical Society and is one of the fullest records of an elite colonial woman's life. In 1989 she became the first woman in the South Carolina Business Hall of Fame.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eliza Lucas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rice grown in tidal swamps, and from the 1740s indigo, developed by <b>Eliza Lucas Pinckney</b>, made the planter elite of the Lowcountry the wealthiest in British America.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-south-carolina">Province of South Carolina</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in British Leeward Islands. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.95,-75.16&amp;year=1768" aria-label="Philadelphia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2699 734 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Eliza Lucas Pinckney"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3149 1004h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3149 1004h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.95,-75.16&amp;year=1768">What was here in 1768? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
